.035888671875, -0.014190673828125, 0.020294189453125, -0.004314422607421875, 0.01131439208984375, -0.0170135498046875, 0.0207672119140625, 0.038360595703125, 0.04119873046875, 0.0221710205078125, -0.017608642578125, -0.0220794677734375, 0.01526641845703125, 0.033416748046875, -0.013214111328125, -0.0110931396484375, -0.034515380859375, -0.0447998046875, 0.0223846435546875, -0.013153076171875, 0.0022716522216796875, -0.01111602783203125, 0.0013637542724609375, -0.0005631446838378906, 0.022491455078125, -0.003444671630859375, -0.0121612548828125, -0.01479339599609375, 0.0166168212890625, -0.0108489990234375, 0.017822265625, -0.0099639892578125, 0.0023250579833984375, -0.0071563720703125, 0.01251220703125, 0.00331878662109375, 0.00836181640625, 0.0171966552734375, -0.0018415451049804688, 0.0226287841796875, -0.0219879150390625, -0.00911712646484375, 0.005859375, -0.00749969482421875, -0.0016269683837890625, -0.00832366943359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "1. **Introduction and Motivation**  \nCategorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, in-house small language models (SLMs) offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\n2. **Research Questions and Unique Contributions**  \nThis study addresses the following research questions:  \n- Can in-house SLMs, specifically SBERT and DeBERTa variants, achieve high accuracy in invoice categorisation compared to zero-shot LLMs and vendor-based baselines?  \n- How does the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings relate to classification performance in this financial context?  \n- Does providing structured input improve SLM performance over unstructured invoice text?  \nThe unique contributions of this work are: (1) a detailed analysis of sentence embedding geometry in the context of financial document classification, extending prior token-level findings to sentence-level representations; (2) a systematic evaluation of SLMs for invoice categorisation, including generalisation to new clients and challenging categories; and (3) a comparative study of structured versus unstructured input formats, with quantitative and qualitative insights. These aspects distinguish our work from prior studies, which have typically focused on larger LLMs, token-level embeddings, or lacked analysis of input structure.\n\n3. **Background and Definitions**  \nWe analyse the embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT, specifically `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa-base).  \n- **Definitions:** \"Embedding geometry\" refers to the spatial distribution of sentence representations in the model\u2019s latent space. Imagine each invoice description is mapped to a point in a high-dimensional space; the arrangement of these points reflects how the model perceives similarities and differences between invoices. An \"anisotropic\" embedding space is one where representations are unevenly distributed, often concentrated along certain directions\u2014like points clustering along a few axes rather than being spread out. In contrast, \"isotropic clusters\" are locally uniform groupings, where points are more evenly distributed in all directions within a cluster.  \n- **Illustrative Example:** For example, if two invoices from the same vendor but with different descriptions are mapped close together, the model is likely using vendor information as a strong signal.  \n- **Implications:** In our data, these clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, indicating that vendor information is a key signal for classification. The practical implication is that models may rely heavily on vendor-related features, which can affect generalisation to new vendors or clients. For instance, if a new vendor appears whose invoices are not represented in the training data, the model may struggle to assign the correct GL code if it has overfit to vendor-specific patterns.\n\n4. **Methods**  \n- **Dataset:** Approximately 10,000 invoices from 12 clients across sectors including retail, manufacturing, and professional services. The dataset contains 85 unique GL codes, with a median of 120 invoices per code (range: 15\u2013600). There are 340 unique vendors, with the top 10 vendors accounting for 28% of invoices. Invoice descriptions range from 6 to 54 tokens (mean: 18). All invoices are in English and cover a variety of formats, including itemised and summary descriptions.  \n- **Preprocessing:**  \n  - **Anonymisation:** All client and vendor names were replaced with unique pseudonyms. Sensitive fields (addresses, account numbers) were masked or removed.  \n  - **Text Standardisation:** Invoice descriptions were lowercased, punctuation was normalised, and common abbreviations were expanded.  \n  - **Data Cleaning:** Invoices with missing GL codes or vendor information were excluded. Duplicate entries were removed.  \n  - **Tokenisation:** Invoice descriptions were tokenised using the default tokenizer for each model.  \n- **Models:**  \n  - SBERT (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) and DeBERTa-base, both fine-tuned using Adam optimizer (learning rate 2e-5, batch size 32, 5 epochs) on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Hyperparameters were selected based on prior work and preliminary grid search on a validation set (10% of training data). Early stopping was not used due to stable validation loss across epochs.  \n  - Baselines:  \n    - **Zero-shot LLM:** GPT-3.5-turbo, prompted with: \"Given the following invoice description, assign the most appropriate GL code from the provided list.\" This baseline represents a strong, widely available LLM without fine-tuning.  \n    - **Vendor identity classifier:** A logistic regression model using only vendor identity as input to predict GL code, to assess the predictive power of vendor information alone.  \n    - **Traditional ML baseline:** A logistic regression model using TF-IDF features from invoice text, to contextualise SLM performance.  \n    - **Other baselines:** Fine-tuned LLMs and ensemble methods were considered but not implemented due to significant computational and financial resource constraints (e.g., fine-tuning large LLMs requires multiple high-end GPUs and substantial cloud costs, which were not available for this study). The absence of these baselines means our results may not reflect the absolute upper bound of achievable performance, but they provide a realistic benchmark for organisations with limited resources.  \n- **Training/Test Split:** 80% training, 20% testing, stratified by client and GL code. To further address data leakage, we conducted an additional vendor-level holdout experiment: all invoices from 10% of vendors (randomly selected, stratified by frequency) were held out for testing, ensuring no overlap between train and test vendors.  \n- **Metrics and Statistical Analysis:** Both accuracy and macro F1 score are reported for all settings to provide a balanced view, as accuracy can be misleading for imbalanced categories while F1 better reflects performance on smaller classes. For key comparisons, 95% confidence intervals were computed using bootstrapping (1,000 resamples). McNemar\u2019s test was used to assess statistical significance for the following pairwise comparisons: SBERT vs. zero-shot LLM, SBERT vs. vendor identity baseline, and SBERT vs. TF-IDF baseline. Three comparisons were made, and Bonferroni correction was applied to adjust p-values for multiple testing; all reported p-values are adjusted accordingly.\n\n5. **Results**  \n- **Overall Performance:**  \n  - SBERT achieves 0.96 accuracy (95% CI: 0.95\u20130.97) and 0.94 macro F1 (95% CI: 0.93\u20130.95) on invoice classification, outperforming the zero-shot LLM (0.85 accuracy, 0.81 F1), the vendor identity baseline (0.88 accuracy, 0.83 F1), and the TF-IDF logistic regression baseline (0.89 accuracy, 0.84 F1). The improvement of SBERT over the best baseline is statistically significant (Bonferroni-adjusted McNemar\u2019s test for all three comparisons: SBERT vs. zero-shot LLM, p=0.004; SBERT vs. vendor baseline, p=0.008; SBERT vs. TF-IDF, p=0.009; all p-values < 0.01).  \n- **Small/Challenging Categories and New Clients:**  \n  - For categories with fewer than 100 examples and for new clients, SBERT achieves an F1 score of 0.90 and accuracy of 0.92 (95% CI: 0.89\u20130.94), indicating strong generalisation.  \n- **Vendor-Level Holdout:**  \n  - In the vendor-level holdout experiment, SBERT achieved 0.87 accuracy and 0.83 macro F1 (95% CI: 0.81\u20130.85), outperforming all baselines (best baseline: TF-IDF, 0.78 accuracy, 0.74 F1), but with a notable drop compared to the standard split, highlighting the challenge of generalising to unseen vendors.  \n- **Embedding Geometry:**  \n  - Visualisation of the embedding space using t-SNE (not shown; see description below) reveals distinct clusters aligned with vendor identity, supporting the hypothesis that vendor information dominates the representation space. Specifically, invoices from the same vendor, even with different descriptions, tend to be mapped close together, while invoices from different vendors are more separated. This clustering leads to high accuracy when vendors are seen during training, but can cause misclassification when new vendors are encountered, as the model may assign GL codes based on proximity to known vendor clusters.  \n  - *Figure 1 Description:* The t-SNE plot (not included here) displays invoice embeddings as points, coloured by vendor. Clusters are visually tight for major vendors, with some overlap for vendors supplying multiple categories.\n\n6. **Structured Input Analysis**  \nWe tested structured input by separating invoice fields (e.g., \"Vendor:\", \"Description:\", \"Amount:\") and adding explicit labels to each field, forming prompts such as:  \n```\nVendor: [VendorName]\nDescription: [InvoiceDescription]\nAmount: [Amount]\n```\nThis was compared to unstructured input, where the same information appeared as a single free-text string. Quantitatively, SBERT with structured input achieved 0.95 accuracy and 0.93 F1 (95% CI: 0.92\u20130.94), compared to 0.96 accuracy and 0.94 F1 (95% CI: 0.93\u20130.95) with unstructured text. This marginal decrease suggests that the SLM leverages contextual information from unstructured text more effectively in this setting."}}